--- body ---
## Context

`getSDTCoverage(state)` (bootstrap.js, currently ~line 615) decides whether a
census is safe to display by testing `state.scanned === state.total`. That
comment, carried over from before ticket 0752, still describes the OLD
scheduler: "the census loop increments `scanned` once per item and nothing
else does, so it only reaches `total` when every item has actually been
reclassified this walk." Under the event-driven scheduler that ticket 0752
shipped, that description is no longer accurate, and the inaccuracy is not
cosmetic -- it is why the denominator moves.

## What the current code actually does

`scheduler.js`'s `record(id, info)` (~line 89) is called both by full
reconciliation and by every single targeted event (an item notification
routed through `dirty` -> `affected()` -> `inspect()` -> `record()`,
~line 160-169), and by the admission loop's own re-checks. Every call ends
with:

```js
state.total = state.scanned = observed.size;
```

Both are set to the SAME value, by construction, on every single call --
not only at the end of a full census. `publish()` (~line 55) treats
`state.scanned === state.total` as "safe to trust and snapshot," and since
`record()` makes that trivially true after literally every event, `publish()`
refreshes `state.censusSnapshot` -- the "last known-COMPLETE classification"
ticket 0753 built specifically to distinguish a genuinely finished walk from
a live, still-rebuilding one -- after every single-item event too, not only
after a reconciliation.

Net effect: `state.total` (the UI's denominator Y) is now live-tracked from
`observed.size`, and updates immediately whenever the library gains or loses
even one attachment -- a new file added, one trashed, a parent item deleted
and cascading its children, a linked file replaced under a new item ID. None
of this needs the ~1-hour reconciliation interval (`RECONCILIATION_INTERVAL_MS`,
SPEC.md 5.2.7) to elapse.

## Reproduction

Using the existing scheduler test fixture shape (`tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`),
loading only `scheduler.js` (no bootstrap.js `affected()` involved -- this is
scheduler-level, not a bootstrap wiring artifact):

1. `host.list()` reports attachments `[1, 2]`; `api.sweep()` completes a full
   census. `state.total === 2`, `state.scanned === 2`.
2. A single targeted event reports a genuinely new attachment: `present.add(3)`,
   `api.invalidate([3])`, `api.pump()`. No reconciliation ran.
   Result: `state.total === 3`, `state.scanned === 3` -- both trivially equal,
   `getSDTCoverage` would call this "complete."
3. A single targeted event reports that attachment 3 is gone again:
   `present.delete(3)`, `api.invalidate([3])`, `api.pump()`.
   Result: `state.total === 2` again.

Denominator sequence across three points `getSDTCoverage` would each call
"complete": `2 -> 3 -> 2`, driven by two ordinary single-item events, with
zero reconciliations in between. Confirmed by running this scenario directly
against `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` (not committed as a test -- this
ticket is investigation, not the fix).

## What this is not

I looked for a way `observed.size` could move WITHOUT a genuine corresponding
change to a real attachment's presence -- a phantom inflation/deflation bug
rather than live tracking of real churn. Two paths in bootstrap.js's
`affected()`/`inspect()` looked suspicious and I could not make either
misbehave:

- A parent-item deletion adds both cached former children (`children.get(id)`)
  and current DB children to the affected set (bootstrap.js ~line 2044). A
  stale cached child that no longer exists resolves through `inspect()`'s
  `!item` branch, which always returns `{status: 'excluded', absent: true}`
  regardless of whether the id was ever an attachment -- and `record()`'s
  `observed.delete(id)` on a key that was never present is a harmless no-op
  (Map semantics). No inflation.
- A non-attachment id can only reach the affected set via `affected()`'s own
  `!item || item.isAttachment()` test (bootstrap.js ~line 2047), which never
  adds an EXISTING non-attachment item's own id -- only a deleted one (which
  again resolves through the `absent: true` branch above) or its real
  attachment children (via a DB query scoped to `itemAttachments`, which by
  definition only returns genuine attachments).

So every `observed.size` change I could construct corresponds to a real
attachment appearing or disappearing from `itemAttachments` (including trash/
restore, since neither the full census's `list()` nor the event path filters
on `deleted` -- consistent between the two, and by design: `outOfScope`
subtracts excluded/unsupported from the coverage total in `getSDTCoverage`
rather than the census itself dropping them). I did not find a defect that
moves Y without a real corresponding library change.

## The open question this leaves

Given a large, actively-synced library (the author's own: 2 600 `unsupported`,
367 `missing-source`), Zotero's own background sync plausibly adds, removes or
replaces attachments continuously and quietly -- which this scheduler will now
report as an immediate Y change, live, rather than holding Y steady until the
next reconciliation. That may be exactly the small real churn the author saw
bouncing. Whether that is wanted (Y as a live, honest reflection of the
library, matching the event-driven ticket 0752's own philosophy) or unwanted
(Y should read as a "census" figure, stable between reconciliation points, with
per-item events only moving the numerator X until the next reconciliation
settles Y) is a product decision, not a bug this ticket can rule on -- the
same shape as ticket 0737's table-vs-derivation arbitration.

If Y-should-be-stable is the ruling: the fix is narrow. `record()`'s
`state.total = state.scanned = observed.size` line would need to stop moving
`total` outside of the full-census branch (~line 136,
`state.total = ids.length`), and `getSDTCoverage`'s "complete" test would need
a real signal for "a reconciliation just finished" distinct from "the
scheduler's bookkeeping happens to be self-consistent right now" -- `record()`
currently gives it none. `state.lastReconciliationAt` (set at ~line 155) is
the nearest existing candidate.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` -- `record()` (~line 89), `publish()`
  (~line 55), the reconciliation branch (~line 126-158)
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` -- `getSDTCoverage()` (~line 615, the
  stale "this walk" comment), `affected()` (~line 2044), `inspect()`
  (~line 1872)
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` -- fixture shape used for the reproduction
  above; a committed version of it would be Action 1 for whoever picks this up

## Exit criteria

Not a fix ticket as filed: the author has ruled whether Y should be live
(matching current behaviour, in which case this ticket closes by correcting
`getSDTCoverage`'s stale comment to describe what "complete" actually means
now) or reconciliation-anchored (in which case a child ticket implements the
narrow fix above). Either way, the denominator's actual contract is written
down somewhere a reader can find it, which today it is not.
